Out-of-tree kernel modules for the Xiaomi Pad 8 Pro
===================================================

  xiaomi-fp-uio-dkms/    The Linux side of the fingerprint sensor.

Why out of tree
---------------

The sensor's SPI bus belongs to TrustZone, so Linux never sees an image or a
template. What is left on this side is a supply, a reset line and an
interrupt - and no subsystem upstream owns a device like that, so the driver
has nowhere to land yet. Keeping it out of tree means the kernel can be a
plain mainline build, and the module can change without rebuilding it.


What it is
----------

A UIO device. read() blocks until the sensor's event count moves, poll() works,
and open()/release() are wired to runtime PM - which is where the supply is
raised and the reset line pulsed. There is no ioctl at all, and no netlink.

Opening it brings the sensor up. Closing it puts the sensor back into reset
with its rail off, including when it closes because the process died, which is
what the ioctl version got wrong.

The interrupt stays this driver's own (UIO_IRQ_CUSTOM) rather than the UIO
core's, because a wake IRQ cannot be handed to the core and the sensor is meant
to wake the machine.

uio_pdrv_genirq would very nearly do instead of a driver at all. It is not
used because binding it means claiming its single of_id module parameter for
this sensor, and then nobody on the machine can use it for anything else.


Installing
----------

    cd xiaomi-fp-uio-dkms && makepkg -f          # not as root
    sudo pacman -U *.pkg.tar.zst

DKMS then rebuilds it for each kernel, which needs the kernel's build tree at
/lib/modules/$(uname -r)/build. On this machine the kernel is cross-built on
another host, so that link points at a directory that is not there; until a
headers package exists, build the module where the kernel was built:

    make KDIR=/tmp/linux/build-piano ARCH=arm64 LLVM=1

LLVM=1 matters - the kernel is built with clang, and gcc will not accept the
flags its build passes down.


Binding it to the shipped device tree
-------------------------------------

The device tree that ships in boot_b calls the sensor "xiaomi,xiaomi-fp",
which the driver still matches. The name it should have is the sensor's own,
"goodix,gf3656zs1"; goodix,gf3656zs1.yaml describes that binding, for the day
the device tree is fixed and for an eventual submission upstream.

If a kernel still has the old drivers/input/misc/xiaomi-side-fingerprint.c
built in, it claims the device first and this module will not bind. To try
this one without rebuilding the kernel:

    echo fingerprint > /sys/bus/platform/drivers/xiaomi-fp/unbind
    modprobe xiaomi-fp-uio


What uses it
------------

libfprint/ - the libfprint driver. It finds the
device by the name in /sys/class/uio/uioN/name, not by a fixed minor.
